01:05:28.920 --> 01:05:35.320
Cole Medin: Oppenheim asked, outside their biases towards using vanilla clawed code, what are the benefits of Archon over clawed loops?

386
01:05:35.830 --> 01:05:38.330
Cole Medin: Yes, that's a good question.

387
01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:44.730
Cole Medin: So, hopefully it doesn't take too long to load this here, but let me go over to the repository for Archon.

388
01:05:46.860 --> 01:05:50.860
Cole Medin: Over to GitHub. Alright, so the main thing is reliability.

389
01:05:51.110 --> 01:06:04.270
Cole Medin: The problem with loops in Cloud Code, or something like a Ralph loop, is you have no control over the actual workflow. Cloud Code itself defines the prompts for each iteration of the loop.

390
01:06:05.320 --> 01:06:22.930
Cole Medin: And so you really don't have much of a say of, like, what skills it's using, how it's validating things. I mean, you can try as much as you can to, like, prompt it up front to set the loop up in that way, but the beauty of Archon workflows is you're defining the process, and you're making it as deterministic and repeatable as possible.

391
01:06:23.130 --> 01:06:36.209
Cole Medin: Because Archon allows you to define these workflows as YAML files, where you set up the stages. Like, this is how I want to plan, and then I want to go into another coding agent to take that plan and do the implementation.

392
01:06:36.210 --> 01:06:48.570
Cole Medin: and then I want to run the tests, but I want to do it deterministically. Like, I'm not letting the LLM decide how to run the tests, because they are non-deterministic, they might get it wrong, or forget some of the tests that you tell it to run.

393
01:06:48.770 --> 01:07:13.729
Cole Medin: And so you're enforcing the test to be run in a certain way right after the implementation. And a lot of these problems of large language models being non-deterministic and forgetting to do things, they're exaggerated by loops, because loops are a longer set of work that you're laying out for it, there's more for it to forget, and there's longer time in between you prompting it, so it's forgetting things that you're telling it to do in the system as a whole, like the orchestrators.

394
01:07:13.730 --> 01:07:20.669
Cole Medin: going to lose track of your original goal, over time. At least that's a risk that we have with loops in general.

395
01:07:20.670 --> 01:07:31.870
Cole Medin: So, so slash loop and Claude Code is cool because of how simple it is, but it's actually pretty limited in its reliability. And using slash loop and clawed code also, by the way, just keeps you in a single session.

396
01:07:32.090 --> 01:07:55.639
Cole Medin: So it's very token inefficient, and you start to get into that dumb zone for the large language model very quickly, because in between loops, it's not resetting the session. So you start to have, like, hundreds and hundreds of thousands of tokens in your context window, and the large language model gets completely overwhelmed with everything that you have. And so, with Archon workflows, we get to set up these independent nodes as separate coding agent sessions that are

397
01:07:55.640 --> 01:08:02.370
Cole Medin: working together through handoff documents that we create. Like, handoffs, in general are just a really big thing in the industry right now.

398
01:08:02.490 --> 01:08:18.410
Cole Medin: Because it's a way to keep your context lean and efficient, right? Like, you have one agent do the planning, and then it creates a plan document, and then you send that into another coding agent that does the implementation, and then it creates the implementation handoff that you then send into the review agent.

399
01:08:18.430 --> 01:08:29.170
Cole Medin: Right? Like, we don't want to do everything all in one coding agent session, because we want to keep each individual agent as focused as possible. That's how we get the most reliable results. Archon helps you with that.

400
01:08:29.450 --> 01:08:42.979
Cole Medin: And then also being able to even, like, do things in parallel. Like, my co-founder can look at a spec for Northstar and decide, like, okay, I want to actually do 3 Archon workflows at the same time, because there are 3 features that I can easily build in parallel.

401
01:08:42.979 --> 01:08:53.109
Cole Medin: Archon supports work trees under the hood, so when you kick off an Archon workflow, it gets its own isolated environment, so that way it's not stepping on the toes of the other

402
01:08:53.109 --> 01:09:00.140
Cole Medin: workflows that are running, like, it has its own local copy of the codebase, so it can work on its own thing, and then create a pull request.

403
01:09:00.140 --> 01:09:08.250
Cole Medin: and not worry about merge conflicts and tests breaking because the other coding agent was doing some edits as well. We definitely want to avoid that kind of thing.

