01:11:20.430 --> 01:11:22.269
Cole Medin: Now, that is a good question.

418
01:11:22.450 --> 01:11:41.460
Cole Medin: And the answer to that is… it goes back to the reliability. Like, sure, everything in this Archon workflow, you could just have this as a single skill file, but then you're asking a single agent to do way too much at once, right? There's no splitting between different coding agent sessions there.

419
01:11:41.890 --> 01:11:48.790
Cole Medin: So the point of Archon is, instead of just one massive skill, we take the workflow, and we build it as a system.

420
01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:52.760
Cole Medin: So, kind of like that little, like, elevator pitch for Archon.

421
01:11:53.560 --> 01:12:08.059
Cole Medin: is, you know, instead of building the system into the coding agent, like, relying on prompts that are brittle and it doesn't always follow, instead we're building the agent into the system. That flip is actually really, really important, because we have more control that way.

422
01:12:08.060 --> 01:12:19.229
Cole Medin: We are deciding where we want the coding agent to run in a workflow versus where we want a deterministic action. And then we also get to decide, like, when do we create a brand new session?

423
01:12:19.230 --> 01:12:26.350
Cole Medin: Versus when do we have a separate step that just continues a conversation? So, it allows us to have a lot more structure.

424
01:12:26.380 --> 01:12:42.150
Cole Medin: in our approach for our AI coding workflow. Like, literally, no matter your AI coding workflow, there is a way for you to package it up as an ERCon workflow, and, so you never have to have, like, a skill drive way too much at once. You're not gonna get as good of results if you do.

